['Here is an example implementation of the C function that manages a collection of 'record' structures, with dynamic resizing and thread-safe updates:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>

// Structure to represent a single record
typedef struct {
    int key;
    int value;
} Record;

// Global variables for the collection of records
static pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
static int num_records = 0;
static Record *records = NULL;

// Function to add a new record to the collection
int add_record(int key, int value) {
    // Lock the mutex to prevent concurrent updates
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    // Check if the record already exists and update it if necessary
    for (int i = 0; i < num_records; i++) {
        if (records[i].key == key) {
            records[i].value = value;
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
            return 1;
        }
    }

    // If the record doesn't exist, add it to the end of the array
    if (num_records == 0) {
        records = (Record *)malloc(sizeof(Record));
    } else {
        records = realloc(records, (num_records + 1) * sizeof(Record));
    }
    records[num_records].key = key;
    records[num_records].value = value;
    num_records++;

    // Unlock the mutex and return success
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
    return 0;
}

// Function to update an existing record in the collection
int update_record(int key, int value) {
    // Lock the mutex to prevent concurrent updates
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    // Check if the record exists and update it if necessary
    for (int i = 0; i < num_records; i++) {
        if (records[i].key == key) {
            records[i].value = value;
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
            return 1;
        }
    }

    // If the record doesn't exist, do nothing and return failure
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
    return 0;
}

// Function to read a record from the collection
int get_record(int key) {
    // Lock the mutex to prevent concurrent updates
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    // Check if the record exists and return it if necessary
    for (int i = 0; i < num_records; i++) {
        if (records[i].key == key) {
            int value = records[i].value;
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
            return value;
        }
    }

    // If the record doesn't exist, return failure
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
    return -1;
}

// Function to remove a record from the collection
int remove_record(int key) {
    // Lock the mutex to prevent concurrent updates
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    // Check if the record exists and remove it if necessary
    for (int i = 0; i < num_records; i++) {
        if (records[i].key == key) {
            for (int j = i; j < num_records - 1; j++) {
                records[j] = records[j + 1];
            }
            num_records--;
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
            return 1;
        }
    }

    // If the record doesn't exist, do nothing and return failure
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
    return 0;
}

// Function to persist the state of the records to disk
void save_records() {
    // Lock the mutex to prevent concurrent updates
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    // Write the record data to a file
    FILE *file = fopen("records.dat", "wb");
    for (int i = 0; i < num_records; i++) {
        Record record = records[i];
        fwrite(&record, sizeof(Record), 1, file);
    }
    fclose(file);

    // Unlock the mutex and return success
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
}

// Function to load the state of the records from disk
void load_records() {
    // Lock the mutex to prevent concurrent updates
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    // Read the record data from a file
    FILE *file = fopen("records.dat", "rb");
    if (file != NULL) {
        int num_records = 0;
        Record record;
        while (fread(&record, sizeof(Record), 1, file)) {
            add_record(record.key, record.value);
            num_records++;
        }
        fclose(file);
    }

    // Unlock the mutex and return success
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
}
```
This implementation uses a single mutex to protect concurrent access to the collection of records, and it dynamically resizes the arrays as needed. It also provides functions for adding, updating, reading, and removing records, as well as for persisting and loading the state of the records from disk.

To ensure thread-safety, the functions use the mutex to lock access to the collection of records while they update or read it. This ensures that multiple threads can safely access and modify the collection without interfering with each other.

The function for adding a new record dynamically resizes the arrays as needed by reallocating memory from the heap. It also ensures that the new record is added to the end of the array, so that it can be accessed efficiently using a single index.

The function for updating an existing record checks if the record exists and updates it if necessary. If the record doesn't exist, it does nothing and returns failure.

The function for reading a record from the collection locks the mutex while accessing the collection to ensure thread-safety. It then searches the array of records for the specified key and returns the corresponding value if it exists. If the record doesn't exist, it returns failure.

The function for removing a record checks if the record exists and removes it if necessary. If the record doesn't exist, it does nothing and returns failure. It also updates the size of the array and shifts the remaining records to fill the gap.

The functions for persisting and loading the state of the records use the mutex to protect concurrent access to the collection while writing or reading the data from disk. They also ensure that the record data is written in a consistent format and that the file is closed properly after being opened.

Overall, this implementation provides a thread-safe and efficient way to manage a collection of records, with functions for adding, updating, reading, and removing records, as well as for persisting and loading the state of the records from disk.']

