### Dynamic Array Management
```scss
struct FSM {
    string path;
    int timestamp;
    int size;
    bool permission;
};

class DynamicArray {
public:
    DynamicArray(int initialCapacity = 10) : capacity_(initialCapacity), size_(0) {}

    void insert(const FSM& fsm) {
        if (size_ >= capacity_) {
            capacity_ *= 2; // Doubles the capacity when full
            elements_.resize(capacity_);
        }

        elements_[size_++] = fsm;
    }

    void remove(int index) {
        if (index < size_) {
            for (int i = index; i < size_; ++i) {
                elements_[i] = std::move(elements_[i + 1]);
            }
            --size_;
        }
    }

    FSM& get(int index) {
        return elements_[index];
    }

private:
    int capacity_, size_;
    std::vector<FSM> elements_;
};
```
### Data Structure Selection

In this implementation, we choose to use a red-black tree as the data structure for managing the FSM entries. This data structure provides efficient lookup, insertion, and deletion operations while maintaining a balanced structure. The trade-off between complexity, performance, and space usage is that red-black trees have a higher space usage compared to other data structures like linked lists or hash tables, but they provide faster lookup and insertion times.
```scss
class FSMTree {
public:
    FSMTree() {}

    void insert(const FSM& fsm) {
        // Insert the FSM entry into the tree
        auto node = std::make_shared<FSMNode>(fsm);
        tree_.insert(node);

        // Update the size of the tree
        ++size_;
    }

    void remove(const FSM& fsm) {
        // Find the FSM entry in the tree and delete it
        auto node = findNode(fsm);
        if (node != nullptr) {
            tree_.erase(node);

            // Update the size of the tree
            --size_;
        }
    }

    bool contains(const FSM& fsm) const {
        // Check if the FSM entry is present in the tree
        return findNode(fsm) != nullptr;
    }

    int size() const {
        // Return the number of FSM entries in the tree
        return size_;
    }

private:
    struct FSMNode : public std::enable_shared_from_this<FSMNode> {
        FSM fsm;

        FSMNode(const FSM& fsm) : fsm(fsm) {}

        bool operator==(const FSMNode& other) const {
            return fsm == other.fsm;
        }
    };

    std::shared_ptr<FSMNode> findNode(const FSM& fsm) {
        // Find the FSM entry in the tree using a binary search algorithm
        auto node = std::make_shared<FSMNode>(fsm);
        auto it = tree_.lower_bound(node);

        if (it != tree_.end() && *it == fsm) {
            return *it;
        } else {
            return nullptr;
        }
    }

    int size_;
    std::set<std::shared_ptr<FSMNode>> tree_;
};
```
### Concurrency Support

To handle concurrent modifications safely, we use a locking mechanism to prevent race conditions and ensure data integrity. We use a shared mutex to synchronize access to the FSM tree during insertion, deletion, and lookup operations. When multiple threads are accessing the same data structure simultaneously, this ensures that only one thread can modify the data structure at any given time, which prevents inconsistencies in the data.
```scss
class FSMLock {
public:
    FSMLock() {}

    void lock() {
        // Lock the mutex to prevent other threads from modifying the FSM tree
        mutex_.lock();
    }

    void unlock() {
        // Unlock the mutex to allow other threads to access the FSM tree
        mutex_.unlock();
    }

private:
    std::mutex mutex_;
};
```
### Memory Optimization

To optimize memory usage, we carefully manage data structures and pointers. We use smart pointers instead of raw pointers to dynamically allocate and deallocate memory, which helps to minimize memory fragmentation. We also use memory pools to reduce the overhead of memory allocation and deallocation. This ensures that memory is allocated efficiently and that the system can handle large datasets without significant performance degradation.
```scss
class MemoryPool {
public:
    MemoryPool(int initialSize = 10) : size_(initialSize), head_(nullptr) {}

    void* allocate() {
        // Allocate memory from the pool
        if (head_ == nullptr || head_->next_ != nullptr) {
            head_ = std::make_shared<MemoryNode>(size_, head_);
        }

        return head_;
    }

    void deallocate(void* ptr) {
        // Deallocate memory from the pool
        auto node = std::static_pointer_cast<MemoryNode>(ptr);
        node->next_ = head_;
        head_ = node;
    }

private:
    struct MemoryNode {
        int size_;
        std::shared_ptr<MemoryNode> next_;

        MemoryNode(int size, const std::shared_ptr<MemoryNode>& next) : size_(size), next_(next) {}
    };

    int size_;
    std::shared_ptr<MemoryNode> head_;
};
```
### Overall Performance

The overall performance of the system is improved by using a locking mechanism to handle concurrent modifications safely, and by optimizing memory usage using smart pointers and memory pools. The use of the red-black tree ensures that lookups are performed efficiently, while the use of the shared mutex ensures that only one thread can modify the FSM tree at any given time, which prevents inconsistencies in the data.']

