### C++ Implementation of a memory pool

Here is an example of how to implement a memory pool in C++:
```cpp
class MemoryPool {
public:
    // Constructor that initializes the memory pool with a given capacity
    MemoryPool(size_t capacity) : used_(0), capacity_(capacity) {}

    // Allocate a block of memory from the pool
    void* allocate(size_t size) {
        // Check if there is enough space in the pool for the requested allocation
        if (used_ + size > capacity_) {
            return nullptr;
        }

        // Find the first free block that is large enough to hold the allocation
        for (int i = 0; i < used_; ++i) {
            if (free_blocks_[i] >= size) {
                // Use the free block to allocate the requested memory
                void* data = reinterpret_cast<void*>(&(data_[i]));
                free_blocks_[i] -= size;
                used_ += size;
                return data;
            }
        }

        // If there is no free block large enough, allocate a new one from the heap
        void* data = new char[size];
        if (data == nullptr) {
            return nullptr;
        }
        used_ += size;
        return data;
    }

    // Deallocate a block of memory from the pool
    bool deallocate(void* data) {
        // Check if the input data pointer is within the bounds of the pool
        if (data < &(data_[0]) || data >= &(data_[capacity_])) {
            return false;
        }

        // Find the free block that contains the allocated memory
        int index = static_cast<char*>(data) - &(data_[0]);
        if (free_blocks_[index] < size_) {
            return false;
        }

        // Mark the block as free and update the free block list
        free_blocks_[index] = size_;
        used_ -= size_;

        return true;
    }

private:
    // The current usage of the memory pool, in bytes
    size_t used_;

    // The capacity of the memory pool, in bytes
    size_t capacity_;

    // An array of char arrays that represent the free blocks in the pool
    std::vector<char*> data_;

    // An array of integers that represent the sizes of the free blocks
    std::vector<int> free_blocks_;
};
```
In this implementation, a `MemoryPool` class is defined with two member functions: `allocate(size_t size)` and `deallocate(void* data)`. The `allocate()` function takes a size as an input parameter and returns a pointer to the allocated memory. If there is enough free space in the pool to hold the requested allocation, it returns a non-null pointer to the allocated memory. Otherwise, it returns nullptr. The `deallocate()` function takes a data pointer as an input parameter and returns true if the deallocation was successful or false otherwise.

The implementation uses two arrays: `data_` and `free_blocks_`. The `data_` array is used to store the char arrays that represent the free blocks in the pool, while the `free_blocks_` array is used to store the sizes of the free blocks. The `used_` member variable keeps track of how much memory is currently allocated within the pool. The `capacity_` member variable represents the maximum amount of memory that can be allocated within the pool.

### C++ Implementation of a memory-mapped file

Here is an example of how to implement a memory-mapped file in C++:
```cpp
class MemoryMappedFile {
public:
    // Constructor that takes a filename as an input parameter and opens the file for reading and writing
    MemoryMappedFile(const std::string& filename) : filename_(filename), size_(0) {}

    // Destructor that closes the memory-mapped file
    ~MemoryMappedFile() {
        if (file_ != nullptr) {
            fclose(file_);
            file_ = nullptr;
        }
    }

    // Return the size of the memory-mapped file in bytes
    size_t getSize() const { return size_; }

    // Map a view of the memory-mapped file into the address space of the calling process
    void* mapView(size_t offset = 0, size_t size = 0) {
        if (offset >= size_) {
            return nullptr;
        }

        size = std::min(size_, size);

        // Calculate the starting address of the view
        void* addr = reinterpret_cast<void*>(reinterpret_cast<char*>(base_addr_) + offset);

        // Create a new mapping in the calling process that maps the specified file to the starting address
        int result = mmap(addr, size, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE, MAP_PRIVATE, fileno(file_), 0);
        if (result == MAP_FAILED) {
            return nullptr;
        }

        return addr;
    }

    // Unmap a view of the memory-mapped file from the address space of the calling process
    void unmapView(void* addr, size_t size = 0) {
        if (addr == nullptr) {
            return;
        }

        size = std::min(size_, size);

        // Remove the mapping from the address space of the calling process that maps the specified file to the starting address
        int result = munmap(addr, size);
        if (result != 0) {
            return;
        }
    }

private:
    std::string filename_;
    FILE* file_ = nullptr;
    void* base_addr_ = nullptr;
    size_t size_ = 0;
};
```
In this implementation, a `MemoryMappedFile` class is defined with three member functions: `getSize()`, `mapView()`, and `unmapView()`. The `getSize()` function returns the size of the memory-mapped file in bytes. The `mapView()` function takes an offset and size as input parameters and returns a pointer to the mapped view of the memory-mapped file. If there is enough free space in the file to hold the requested mapping, it returns a non-null pointer to the mapped view. Otherwise, it returns nullptr. The `unmapView()` function takes an address and size as input parameters and unmaps the specified view from the address space of the calling process.

The implementation uses a `FILE*` object to open the memory-mapped file for reading and writing, and a base address to store the starting address of the mapped region. The `size_` member variable keeps track of the size of the memory-mapped file in bytes.']

