The `init_mac_transmissions()` function initializes the dynamic array with the given size, setting all fields to invalid values. The `add_mac_transmission()` function adds a new transmission to the dynamic array by checking if there is enough space in the array for the new transmission. If there is not enough space, the oldest transmission is removed and the new one is added.

The `get_mac_transmission()` function retrieves a `MAC_Transmission_T` structure from the dynamic array with the given sequence number. The function iterates through the array until it finds the sequence number or reaches the end of the array, returning NULL if the sequence number is not found.

The `get_rb_grant()` function retrieves a `RB_GRANT_T` element from the dynamic array with the given grant ID. The function iterates through the array until it finds the grant ID or reaches the end of the array, returning NULL if the grant ID is not found.

The solution accounts for network jitter and potential delays in data availability when designing the scheduler. It also considers trade-offs between response time and resource usage and ensures the solution is robust against failures in the external data source.']

