// Print a geometric shape structure in the memory pool
void print_geometric_shapes(struct mempool_list *mempool, int type) {
  struct geometric_shape *shape = NULL;
  // Find the shape with the specified type in the linked list of shapes
  for (struct mempool_node *node = mempool->head; node != NULL; node = node->next) {
    if (node->shape.type == type) {
      shape = &(node->shape);
      break;
    }
  }
  // Print the shape structure
  if (shape != NULL) {
    printf("Shape with type %d:\n", shape->type);
    printf("\tDimensions: [%d, %d, %d]\n", shape->dimensions[0], shape->dimensions[1], shape->dimensions[2]);
  } else {
    printf("Shape with type %d not found\n", type);
  }
}

int main() {
  struct mempool_list *mempool = (struct mempool_list *)malloc(sizeof(struct mempool_list));
  if (!mempool) {
    printf("Allocation failed\n");
    return -1;
  }
  // Initialize the memory pool with default values
  initialize_geometric_shapes(mempool, 0);

  int type = 0;
  while (type != -1) {
    printf("Enter a shape type (or -1 to exit): ");
    scanf("%d", &type);
    if (type == -1) {
      break;
    }
    // Insert a geometric shape structure into the memory pool
    insert_geometric_shapes(mempool, type);
  }

  printf("Memory pool contents:\n");
  for (struct mempool_node *node = mempool->head; node != NULL; node = node->next) {
    print_geometric_shapes(mempool, node->shape.type);
  }

  return 0;
}
```
This program creates a memory pool for storing geometric shapes with dimensions as arrays of integers. The user can add and delete shapes from the memory pool using the functions `insert_geometric_shapes` and `delete_geometric_shapes`. The user can also modify existing shapes in the memory pool using the function `modify_geometric_shapes`. Finally, the program prints all of the shapes currently stored in the memory pool.

The main difference between this example and the previous one is that now we are storing the shapes in a linked list data structure, which allows us to add and delete shapes efficiently without having to reallocate the entire memory block every time a shape is added or deleted. We also have to handle the case where a shape with a given type does not exist in the memory pool, which requires an additional condition in the functions `insert_geometric_shapes` and `delete_geometric_shapes`.

Another important aspect of this program is that we are using thread-safe functions for allocating and deallocating memory. The function `malloc` and `free` are not thread-safe, so we use the functions `pthread_mutex_lock` and `pthread_mutex_unlock` to protect the critical section of code where these functions are used. This ensures that only one thread can access the memory pool at a time, which is important for avoiding race conditions when multiple threads try to modify the memory pool simultaneously.

In this example, we have learned how to use mutex locks and conditional variables to protect critical sections of code from concurrent access by multiple threads. We also learned how to create a linked list data structure that allows us to add and delete shapes efficiently without reallocating the entire memory block every time a shape is added or deleted.']

