This will create an executable file called `file_compression` that you can run by typing `./file_compression`.

If you want to compress a file, you can use the following command:

`./file_compression -c <input_filename>`

Replace `<input_filename>` with the name of the file you want to compress. The compressed file will have the same name as the original file but with an additional `.gz` extension.

If you want to decompress a file, you can use the following command:

`./file_compression -d <input_filename>`

Replace `<input_filename>` with the name of the file you want to decompress. The decompressed file will have the same name as the original file but without the `.gz` extension.

If you want to read a compressed file, you can use the following command:

`./file_compression -r <input_filename>`

Replace `<input_filename>` with the name of the file you want to read. The contents of the file will be printed to the terminal or command prompt.

If you want to write a compressed file, you can use the following command:

`./file_compression -w <output_filename>`

Replace `<output_filename>` with the name of the file you want to write. The contents of the file will be read from standard input and written to the compressed file.

Note that the `gunzip` command is used for decompressing files and the `gzip` command is used for compressing files.']

