}
```
# Background Processing

### System Command Execution

To execute a system command as a background process, we can use the `fork()` and `execl()` functions to create a new process that executes the specified command. We can redirect the standard output of the command to a log file using the `dup2()` function, which allows us to separate the output from the main application flow without affecting performance.
```c
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <unistd.h>

void spawn_background_process(const char *command) {
    pid_t pid = fork();
    if (pid == 0) {
        dup2(open("logfile.txt", O_RDWR | O_CREAT, 0644), STDOUT_FILENO);
        execl("/bin/sh", "sh", "-c", command, NULL);
    } else if (pid > 0) {
        // parent process
    } else {
        perror("fork");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }
}
```
# Performance Measurement

### Packet Generation Rate

To measure the packet generation rate, we can use a timer to track the time it takes for each iteration of the main loop. We can then calculate the average time per packet and divide by the number of packets to get the average packet generation rate in seconds.
```c
#include <time.h>

void measure_performance() {
    clock_t start = clock();
    for (int i = 0; i < num_packets; ++i) {
        populate_packets(&packets[i], 1);
        spawn_background_process("some_command");
    }
    clock_t end = clock();
    double time_per_packet = (double)(end - start) / CLOCKS_PER_SEC;
    double avg_time_per_packet = time_per_packet / num_packets;
    printf("Packet generation rate: %f packets/s\n", 1 / avg_time_per_packet);
}
```
