The function uses a real-time filtering mechanism to evaluate incoming network transactions based on their age, frequency within the last five seconds, and a weighted sum calculation. The function maintains a set of metrics, including the maximum weighted sum and the corresponding transaction ID, while ensuring the integrity of the transaction pool.

The function handles up to 15,000 transactions per second, with each transaction requiring approximately 80 bytes of memory. Transactions are processed in a thread-safe manner using a lock mechanism to prevent race conditions. Additionally, the function logs any invalid transactions or checksum errors for debugging purposes. To manage high transaction rates, the function considers implementing a priority queue to prioritize transactions based on their age and weight.

The function also takes into consideration the implications of choosing between different synchronization mechanisms and their impact on performance and correctness. In this case, it chooses a mutex for simple locking and avoids using more advanced concurrent data structures like reader-writer locks for better throughput under heavy load. Additionally, assessing the overhead of maintaining a separate priority queue versus integrating sorting into the existing processing pipeline is also considered.

Finally, the function considers the scalability of its solution when integrating third-party libraries for external network traffic analysis, ensuring that these integrations do not introduce bottlenecks or compatibility issues.']

